all works.\n\nActually, given the environment may not have internet, let me build with the standard library `http.server`. This is the safest approach - it will always build and tests will pass since there are no external dependencies. But that's more work for routing.\n\nAlternatively, I could try to install Flask. Let me try a quick pip install to see if internet is available. But that could be risky. Let me think about the best approach.\n\nActually, the most robust approach given uncertainty about internet access is to use Python's built-in `http.server` module which is always available. But that makes routing harder. Let me consider using the `wsgiref` or just building a simple handler.\n\nActually, let me just try to install Flask - if it works great, if not I'll fall back to standard library. Let me first try.\n\nActually, thinking about it more - the task says \"Use the specified language and framework\" and framework is \"unknown\". This means I get to choose. The safest choice that will always build and pass tests is the standard library. Let me build a clean REST API using `http.server` from the standard library with SQLite. This has zero external dependencies and will definitely work.\n\nBut wait - using http.server makes the test code a bit more complex (need to start the server, make HTTP requests). With Flask it's easier with the test client. But Flask needs to be installed.\n\nLet me try installing Flask first. If it works, I'll use Flask which is more idiomatic for a REST API. If not, I'll fall back to stdlib.\n\nLet me try.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0631e375f001ZIUDNHQx2Vhdt8","sessionID":"ses_f9ce21c9dffeUMalt2vbnwe2Vc","messageID":"msg_0631e1007001UyapThwHhe6Duu"}}
